## Known flake (pre-existing, not from this branch)

One run of `cargo test --all --all-features` failed
`service::device::test::lan_carry_over_preserves_iot_mode_observation_time`.
It then passed in isolation and 15/15 in a serial rerun with no other cargo
process on the box, so the failing assertion was never captured. Both the test
and `Device::device_state()` are unchanged on this branch and present on
`origin/main`. Suspected cause and the deterministic repro needed to confirm it
are recorded in TODOS.md rather than guessed at here.

Measurement note, because a review pass reported a much higher rate: several
tests bind fixed ports (`lan_api.rs` uses 4002/4003, and its loopback scenarios
are deliberately folded into ONE test "to avoid a bind race under the parallel
harness"). That guard holds within a test binary but not across concurrent
`cargo test` *processes*. A run of 15 taken while two other reviewers were also
running the suite showed 7 failures, all `AddrInUse` on 4003; the same 15 run
serially showed 0. Any flake rate quoted for this crate is only meaningful with
nothing else building or testing at the same time.

## Pre-merge review

This diff was reviewed for concurrency correctness, test coverage, and doc
accuracy. Nothing blocking. Two defects were found and fixed here:

- The entity advertised `payload_reset: "None"` while the command handler
  parsed every payload as a number, so Home Assistant could never reset it.
  Found while fact-checking a doc line that claimed the command topic accepted
  the reset payload.
- The add-on wiring guard matched raw file text, so commenting out the whole
  `music_palette` block in run.sh left it green. Both fixes ship with a
  mutation-verified regression test: reverting each fix makes exactly one named
  test fail.

Deferred with reasons in TODOS.md: no test asserts the MQTT router's route
table (pre-existing, affects all 17 routes, needs a broker or a refactor), and
`GOVEE_DISABLE_EFFECTS` can leave the slider live with no effect to apply to.

## Not covered here

An earlier revision of this diff was exercised with a real Govee account,
an isolated Mosquitto broker, and a physical H60B0 light (device identifier
redacted). Sensitivity 5 followed by `Music: Rhythm` reached the
Platform API as 5 with code 200; sensitivity 95 followed by the same effect reached
it as 95 with code 200. MQTT retained 95 as the final echo, and direct LAN plus
Home Assistant state confirmed the lamp was restored to OFF. The user visually
confirmed an aggressive music-reactive blink.

That run was made at source hash `9c7471fd2579e92f1eb5c21409c5102d`
`c2ba335487835a73362c77a75d0f072e`, which is NOT the hash at the top of this
file. Two commits landed afterwards, from the pre-merge review:

  - `fix(music): accept the reset payload we advertise to Home Assistant`
  - `test(addon): match run.sh wiring against live shell, not comments`

The second touches only a test. The first adds a new branch to the sensitivity
command path for the exact payload `None`. The numeric path the hardware run
exercised (5 and 95) is behaviourally unchanged: `parse_music_sensitivity` is
untouched, and the new enum's `Set` arm performs the same store under the same
device permit. So the hardware evidence still covers the numeric
path it actually tested, and covers nothing about the reset branch, which has
been verified only in tests. Do not read the current header hash as hardware-
bound; it binds the local gates above.

The reverse-engineered `set-music-palette` LAN command and a deployed Home
Assistant add-on image were not exercised. The visible difference between
sensitivity 5 and 95 was not quantified, so the physical claim is exact command
transport plus visible music-mode reaction, not a measured animation-amplitude
comparison.
